[18:27:48] HARNESS: try 1: 19.2 s, exit 0, answer '{"ideas": [{"side": "shop", "title": "Fast fixed-price close at the market median", "what": "For lamp, chair and bookshelf, open at the current going median (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) with a clear \'fixed price, already our lowest, reply with exactly N\' message, instead of a high opening ask with concessions. Keep the reserve+5 floor in code. Buyers who offer at or above N get an instant deal, with no negotiation rounds.", "evidence": "The top shops by share sit at the median: 11im (19.5%, lamp 100, bookshelf 262) and lee-sungsoo (19.1%, lamp 100, chair 180). nocha12 and 25620027 use \'Fixed price... reply with exactly 260\'. We have 1.5% share with 758 visits. Reviews of other shops praise \'quick deal (1s)\'. Our own 4* review says \'quick deal (1s), receipt matched\'.", "expected": "More deals per visit and a higher share, since buyers skip shops above the upper quartile. Average price per deal drops a few credits, but volume should rise. Stars should hold at about 4.4 or higher.", "risk": "We give up margin from buyers who would have paid more. Our reserve+5 floor may leave little room if the median falls further.", "honest": "The price is the real price we will accept, and the message states a true fixed price. There is no false \'was\' price and no scarcity claim.", "test": "A/B by 15-minute meta_shop window or by item. Run fixed-at-median on one item and the current ask on the others for 24h. Compare deals/visits, share and average price.", "effort": "knob"}, {"side": "shop", "title": "Factual receipt summary that reads as a review", "what": "In the post-deal message, restate the facts in one line a buyer can copy: item, agreed price, that the signed receipt matches the final offer and order. Keep the honest-review request, and ask the buyer to say what they actually found, whatever the rating. Do not offer anything in return and do not condition anything on a review.", "evidence": "The top reviews are factual: 11im\'s \'receipt matched the final offer and ordered item, and the seller signature...\' and ggari-shop\'s \'Deal at 180 for a chair (asked 211) after 5 offers, signed receipt\'. We have 480 reviews at 4.39 stars, below dragonash 4.5 and 11im 4.49. Three of our reviews are 3* for \'quality didn\'t impress me\' and one is a 3* \'No deal\'.", "expected": "Buyer reviews become more informative about receipt integrity, which helps the buyers that choose by reviews. A small lift in stars is possible. No effect on price.", "risk": "A buyer may ignore it. The 3* \'quality\' reviews seem to be a template from a competitor and may not respond to anything we do.", "honest": "We only state facts that are on the receipt. We do not write the review, do not pay for it, and do not make anything depend on it.", "test": "Compare mean stars and the share of reviews that mention \'receipt matched\' for 200 deals before and after the change.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Open at the going median and walk after one counter when the shop is above the quartile", "what": "Set the buyer\'s first offer to the 3-hour median for the item (lamp 100, chair 180, bookshelf 260) rather than a lower fraction of budget. Only continue to a second offer if the shop\'s counter is within the middle half. Otherwise walk and move to the next shop. Keep the rule of at most 40% of the gap per offer.", "evidence": "Our buyer has 8 of 40 trades with no deal, and 5 of the 8 ended in \'walked\' on chair or bookshelf, for example lee-sungsoo chair budget 160 and nocha12 bookshelf budget 230 (twice). The median is 180 for chairs and 260 for bookshelves, so those budgets were below every price. Shops like nocha12 say \'Fixed price, already our lowest\' and 25620027 \'Fixed minimum: 260; no lower bids\', so repeated low offers never succeed.", "expected": "Fewer wasted turns and fewer failed trades. Average spend would stay near 83.6% of budget or rise slightly where prices are fixed. Deal rate should rise because a median opening gets accepted by shops that never move.", "risk": "We overpay by a few credits against shops that would have conceded. Budget-limited buyers still cannot get a deal.", "honest": "The offer is a true offer within budget and within our price cap. Nothing is claimed about other shops or deadlines.", "test": "Log deal rate, price paid and budget fraction for 40 trades per arm and compare against the current opening rule.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Pre-screen budget against shop\'s posted floor before the first offer", "what": "Before spending turns, check each candidate shop\'s last public median for the item (from the ledger). If the budget is below the median of every shop, skip the negotiation, do not make three offers, and log it as \'no deal: budget below market\'. If any shop\'s median is at or below the budget, go there first.", "evidence": "Budget cases that failed: 11im lamp budget 90 (lamp median is 100), dragonash chair budget 225 with TASK_STATE_REJECTED, nocha12 bookshelf budget 230 (twice, median 260), lee-sungsoo chair budget 160 (median 180). Reviews in our own shop show the same: \'No deal: my offers [] stayed within my budget 90\'. Round 2 shops quote \'floor\' prices at or near the median.", "expected": "Cuts the number of failed negotiations and the rate-limit exposure that produced 429 errors on three of our reviews. No change in price paid for the trades that do close.", "risk": "The ledger medians lag the market, so we may skip a shop that has since dropped its price. Skipping all shops produces no deal where a lucky concession might have worked.", "honest": "It uses public ledger data only. We never offer more than budget and never misstate our budget or other buyers.", "test": "Count wasted offers per trade and the \'no deal\' share over 40 trades, and compare it with the previous 40.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
