euichann state a 260 minimum. Our review: \'bookshelf at 275 within budget 325\'. Our share is 1.5% against 19% for lee-sungsoo.", "expected": "Share should rise because buyers that skip shops above the upper quartile will no longer skip us on bookshelves; the price per deal falls by up to ~15 on that item.", "risk": "If reserve + 5 is above 260 for some orders, we simply lose those deals; thin margin.", "honest": "Only lowers our own price and stays above reserve; no claims are made.", "test": "Run meta_shop.py with the new quartile targets for one round window and compare bookshelf deal count per visit against the last window.", "effort": "knob"},\n{"side": "buyer", "title": "Open at the going median and stop raising past it", "what": "Start the buyer\'s first offer at about 90-92% of the going median per item (bookshelf ~240, chair ~165, lamp ~92) and cap the final price at the upper quartile of the middle half (bookshelf 265, chair 182, lamp 103), instead of using the budget as the anchor. Keep the 40% raise per offer but use the median as the ceiling, so the budget only matters when it is below the median.", "evidence": "Our buyer paid 85.3% of budget on average over 32 of 40 trades. Reviews of other shops show deals at the going price regardless of budget: lee-sungsoo \'Chair at 180, budget 185\', 11im \'chair at 195 within budget 250\', JangSeongWoong \'Paid 260.0 of 295\'. Buyers paying 195 for a 180 chair are overpaying.", "expected": "Lower average price paid; fewer cases where the budget is wasted on shops asking above the median such as 11im\'s chair at 185-195.", "risk": "Some shops hold a floor above our cap and the deal fails.", "honest": "We never offer above the budget and write factual reviews of what we paid.", "test": "Log price paid as a share of the median for 40 trades and compare against the 85.3%-of-budget baseline and the no-deal count.", "effort": "small code"},\n{"side": "buyer", "title": "Do not retry shops that failed on the same budget", "what": "Add a short memory rule: if a shop returned no deal for an item at a given budget, skip that shop for that item and budget for a few hours and go to the next one ranked by reviews, instead of trying it again. For lamps at budget 90, go straight to shops whose lamp floor is at or below 90 if any are in the ledger; otherwise report no deal at once.", "evidence": "Our buyer\'s no-deal list: \'11im lamp budget 90 (None)\' three times in a row and \'JangSeongWoong lamp budget 90 (walked)\'. The market lamp median is 100 (middle half 100-103), so a budget of 90 is below every going price, and the repeats cost three trades.", "expected": "Fewer wasted trades; no-deal count from 8 in 40 trades falls toward the number that is truly unreachable.", "risk": "A shop may lower its price later and we would miss that for a few hours.", "honest": "No claims or pressure are involved; it only avoids pointless repeat visits.", "test": "Count repeated visits to the same shop with the same item and budget before and after, and compare the no-deal rate.", "effort": "small code"}\n]}'
