onash 8063/10220 = 79%.", "expected": "Fewer negotiation rounds and fewer buyers who stall or walk after three offers. Deal rate could move from about 70% toward 75-79%, which means more share.", "risk": "Buyers who would have haggled lower pay the stated price. This is minor, because the stated price is already our accept price.", "honest": "N is a real price the shop will honor, and the receipt is signed at N. There is no urgency or scarcity claim.", "test": "A/B by visit parity for 6h each. Measure deal rate per visit and the average number of messages per deal.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Stop re-visiting shops that already caused a walk", "what": "Add a per-shop, per-item memory of walks. After one walk caused by the budget being below the shop\'s floor for that item, put that shop on a cooldown for that item (e.g. 3 hours, or until its reported median moves). In the meantime, route the buyer to shops whose medians fit the budget, such as dragonash or lee-sungsoo for lamps at 100.", "evidence": "Buyer walks: fair-furniture chair budget 160 walked three times, plus fair-furniture lamp 90, dragonash chair 160. Chair going price is 180 (middle half 180-183), so a 160 budget is below every shop. 9 of 40 trades ended without a deal.", "expected": "Saves wasted trips and calls, raises deals from 31/40, and avoids repeat bad outcomes. It does not change the average paid fraction (84.1% of budget).", "risk": "A shop may lower its price later, and the cooldown would miss it. Some budgets are unreachable anywhere, so no routing helps.", "honest": "It only changes which shop we pick, using our own memory and public data. It stays inside budget and does not touch other agents\' load.", "test": "Compare the share of buyer trades ending in a deal, and the number of visits per deal, over the next 40 trades against the last 40.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Anchor first offer at the shop\'s quartile-low and use fewer concessions on cheap shops", "what": "Open at about 95% of the item\'s going median (chair 171, bookshelf 247, lamp 95) when the buyer\'s budget allows, and keep the 40% gap raise rule. Where the budget is below the going median, skip negotiation at once and pick a different shop instead of spending three offers.", "evidence": "Buyer pays 84.1% of budget on average. dragonash counters 255 with 260, 90 with 100 and 180 with 181, so openings 3-10% below median still reach a deal at median within a round or two. ggari-shop review: \'Deal at 180 for a chair (asked 200) after 4 offers\', so a patient, lower opener got a below-ask price.", "expected": "Lower average price paid, perhaps 1-3% on deals where shops sit above the median. Deal count stays flat for shops that counter near median.", "risk": "Low openings can cost an extra round with rigid shops, and a budget-limited buyer may hit the three-offer limit.", "honest": "All offers are real offers within budget. No claim is made about other prices, and the review we write stays factual.", "test": "Run on half of buyer tasks for 40 trades and compare the paid-to-median ratio and deal rate with the control half.", "effort": "knob"}]}'
